# Variables for a Railway (or any PaaS) deployment of LibreSesh.
#
# These cannot live in railway.json: that file carries build and deploy
# settings only, and both variables and volumes are service-level resources the
# platform owns. Set them in the service's Variables tab.
#
# The server checks all of this at boot and refuses to start with a list of
# what is wrong and how to fix it — so if you skip this file, the first deploy
# will simply tell you what it needs.

# --- Required -------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs the identity cookie — it does not encrypt it. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# Set it once and keep it: changing it signs every attendee out at once and
# strands their display names, which stay held by the identities they just
# lost. Store it here and nowhere else — in particular not on the /data volume,
# because the database holds the identity tokens this secret signs, and the two
# halves leaking together is the case that matters.
# (See ARCHITECTURE.md §What a cookie is, exactly.)
COOKIE_SECRET=

# Gates creating events on this instance and downloading the encrypted
# whole-database backup. NOT an event password, and it grants nothing inside
# any event.
INSTANCE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Rate limiting sees the platform's proxy as the only client without this, so
# one visitor can lock out everyone.
TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- Also required: a volume, which is not a variable ----------------------
#
# Attach a volume to the service with mount path exactly /data.
# Without it the SQLite database lives in the container's own filesystem and is
# destroyed on the next deploy — the app looks perfectly healthy right up until
# the rebuild takes the schedule with it. The boot check refuses to start
# rather than let that happen.
#
# DATABASE_PATH and SERVE_STATIC are already set by deploy/Dockerfile
# (/data/app.db and 1). Leave PORT alone — the platform injects it.

# --- Optional -------------------------------------------------------------

# The About page names the deployed commit either way: the server reads
# RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA at runtime. Railway forwards variables set here to the
# image build too, so this reference stamps the bundle itself as well — the
# platform's own variables are not forwarded, which is why it needs saying.
# BUILD_COMMIT=${{RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA}}

# Creates the DemoConf sample event at boot when it is missing — and LongConf
# too when DEMO_MODE is on. Set 0 on a real conference instance, where a fake
# event on the landing page is just noise.
# SEED_DEMO_EVENT=0

# Makes this a demo instance. Two effects: the gate becomes a role picker on
# the seeded demo events — no password checked, anyone can take any role,
# organiser included — and the boot seed adds the fortnight-long LongConf
# beside DemoConf, which is what exercises tracks and the week rail.
# Scoped to those two events: anything else here, including an event created
# through the UI, still checks its passwords.
# DEMO_MODE=1

# Which slugs DEMO_MODE opens up. Defaults to the two seeded fixtures; needed
# only if you seed your own demo under a different slug.
# DEMO_EVENT_SLUGS=democonf-2026,longconf-2026

# Permits a database directory that is not a mounted volume. Only for a
# deliberately disposable instance — a preview build or a throwaway demo.
# ALLOW_EPHEMERAL_DB=1
